You can use the following code to perform a quicksort operation on a list of numbers in JavaScript:

```javascript
function quickSort(arr) {
    if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;
    const pivot = arr[arr.length - 1];
    const left = [];
    const right = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < pivot) {
            left.push(arr[i]);
        } else {
            right.push(arr[i]);
        }
    }

    return [...quickSort(left), pivot, ...quickSort(right)];
}
```

This code defines a recursive function named `quickSort` that takes an array `arr` as input. The function first checks if the length of the array is less than or equal to 1. If so, it simply returns the array as it is already sorted.

If the array has more than 1 element, the function selects the last element of the array as the pivot. It then creates two empty arrays, `left` and `right`, to store the elements that are less than the pivot and greater than or equal to the pivot, respectively.

